Startup Kit — Third-Party Notices
=================================

This file records third-party sources this project draws on, and the limits of
what the MIT license in LICENSE actually covers.


1. Case data and published frameworks
--------------------------------------

The six skills (`forge`, `preflight`, `blueprint`, `pricing`, `compass`, `gtm`)
extract patterns from publicly available founder case data and published
frameworks. None of that source material is redistributed here — the skills
contain derived patterns, benchmarks, and worked examples written in this
project's own words.

Case data and post-mortem collections referenced:

    StarterStory          founder interviews
    IndieHackers          founder interviews and revenue reports
    Y Combinator          Startup School material
    MicroConf             bootstrapper talks
    CB Insights           startup post-mortem analyses
    LOOTR                 startup failure heatmap
    Killed by Google      discontinued product index

Published frameworks referenced:

    Helmer's 7 Powers               moat taxonomy
    The Mom Test (Rob Fitzpatrick)  customer-conversation method
    Hormozi Value Equation          pricing value framing
    Western Electric rules          control-chart rules

These names identify sources. They are used descriptively. No affiliation,
sponsorship, or endorsement by any of the above is implied, and the MIT license
in LICENSE grants no rights to any third-party work.

Individual figures quoted in the skills (revenue numbers, failure rates,
conversion benchmarks) are attributed inline to their source. Where a figure
came from a single source it is marked as not cross-verified. Each skill
records its own sourcing in `references/data-sources.md`.


2. Limits of the derived claims
--------------------------------

The skills are pattern extraction from historical outcomes. Two consequences
are worth stating plainly:

- **Survivorship bias is real.** Success patterns come from founders who chose
  to publish their numbers — a self-selected group. Each skill documents this
  in its `references/data-sources.md`.
- **Case data ages.** A benchmark captured at one time does not hold forever.
  Where the collection date is known, the skills state it.

Nothing in this repository is financial, legal, or investment advice.


3. Named products and standards
